Sure, I can help you create a simple, minimalist homepage with placeholder navigation links for your future experiments. Below is a basic HTML and CSS setup for such a page:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Minimalist Homepage</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
            color: #333;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
        }
        .container {
            text-align: center;
        }
        nav {
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        nav a {
            margin: 0 15px;
            text-decoration: none;
            color: #333;
            font-weight: bold;
        }
        nav a:hover {
            color: #007BFF;
        }
        h1 {
            font-size: 2.5em;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }
        p {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            color: #666;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <nav>
            <a href="#home">Home</a>
            <a href="#calculator">Calculator</a>
            <a href="#tarot">Tarot Deck Simulator</a>
            <a href="#weather">Weather App</a>
            <a href="#pong">Pong</a>
        </nav>
        <h1>Welcome to SpiritOS</h1>
        <p>Your minimalist hub for experiments and applications.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- **HTML Structure**: The HTML includes a simple structure with a navigation bar and a welcome message.
- **CSS Styling**: The CSS is designed to be light and minimalist, with a focus on clean typography and spacing. The body is centered using flexbox, and the navigation links change color on hover for a simple interactive effect.
- **Placeholder Links**: The navigation links are placeholders (`href="#..."`) that you can later replace with actual URLs or JavaScript functions as you develop your applications.

You can expand upon this template by adding more styles or JavaScript functionality as needed for your experiments.
